        {
          "subsection_id": "diathermy_electrocautery_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "1. Introduction and definition",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "diathermy_electrocautery_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Diathermy is the use of high-frequency electrical energy to generate heat in tissue for surgical cutting, coagulation, desiccation or fulguration."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "In modern operating theatres, the term diathermy commonly refers to an electrosurgical unit (ESU) that uses high-frequency alternating current to cut tissue or achieve haemostasis."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "Important OSCE distinction:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Electrosurgery (diathermy): High-frequency electrical current passes through tissue and generates heat within it.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Electrocautery: An electrically heated element transfers heat to tissue; electrical current does not pass through the patient's tissue as part of the cautery mechanism.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Although the terms are often used interchangeably in clinical conversation, they describe different physical mechanisms."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "diathermy_electrocautery_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "2. Identification and components",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "diathermy_electrocautery_group_3",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/diathermy_electrocautery_01.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A. Electrosurgical generator"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The main machine that generates high-frequency electrical energy. It usually has power controls, operating-mode selection and connections for the active instrument and, where required, the patient return electrode."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/diathermy_electrocautery_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "B. Active electrode / diathermy pencil"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The hand-held instrument that delivers electrical energy to the target tissue. Common electrode shapes include a blade, needle and ball electrode."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/diathermy_electrocautery_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "C. Patient return electrode"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A dispersive pad used in conventional monopolar electrosurgery to return current safely from the patient to the generator. It is not required in the same way for bipolar electrosurgery."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/diathermy_electrocautery_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "D. Bipolar forceps"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Two electrodes at the tips of the forceps deliver current through the tissue held between them, allowing relatively localised energy delivery."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "diathermy_electrocautery_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "3. Labelling: what to identify in an OSCE",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "diathermy_electrocautery_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Component",
                    "Function"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Generator",
                      "Produces and regulates the high-frequency electrical output."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Mode selector",
                      "Selects an available cutting or coagulation mode."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Power control",
                      "Sets the requested output level."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Active electrode",
                      "Delivers energy to the target tissue."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Handpiece switches or foot pedal",
                      "Activates the selected output, depending on the system."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Connecting cable",
                      "Connects the handpiece to the generator."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Return electrode",
                      "Completes the monopolar electrical circuit."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Bipolar forceps",
                      "Deliver energy between the two tips."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Return-electrode contact monitor, if fitted",
                      "Detects certain problems with return-pad contact and may interrupt output."
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The exact components vary by manufacturer and by whether the system is monopolar, bipolar or a combined electrosurgical platform."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "diathermy_electrocautery_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "4. Classification of diathermy",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "diathermy_electrocautery_group_5",
              "group_title": "A. Based on the electrical circuit",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/diathermy_electrocautery_07.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "1. Monopolar diathermy"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Current travels from the generator through the active electrode, through the patient's tissue, and returns through the dispersive return electrode."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Uses: General surgery, laparoscopic surgery with compatible instruments, and many open surgical procedures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/diathermy_electrocautery_08.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "2. Bipolar diathermy"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Current flows between the two electrodes, usually the tips of bipolar forceps, through the tissue held between them."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Uses: Neurosurgery, microsurgery, ENT, gynaecology and other procedures requiring localised coagulation."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "diathermy_electrocautery_group_6",
              "group_title": "B. Based on the intended tissue effect",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Cutting: Produces rapid tissue division using concentrated electrical energy.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Coagulation: Produces protein denaturation and haemostasis.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Blend: Combines cutting with a coagulative effect, depending on the generator's design.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Fulguration: Produces superficial tissue destruction through electrical sparks.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Desiccation: Dehydrates tissue through direct electrode contact.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "These settings and effects vary with the waveform, power, electrode geometry, contact time and tissue characteristics."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "diathermy_electrocautery_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "5. Principles of operation",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "diathermy_electrocautery_group_7",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Electrosurgical generators deliver high-frequency alternating current. The high frequency helps avoid the nerve and muscle stimulation associated with ordinary low-frequency electrical current."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "When current passes through tissue, electrical resistance generates heat."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/diathermy_electrocautery_09.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "Where:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "(I) = current.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "(R) = tissue resistance.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "(t) = duration of energy application.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "This relationship is a simplified model; actual tissue heating also depends on current density, waveform, electrode geometry, cooling and changing tissue impedance."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "diathermy_electrocautery_group_8",
              "group_title": "How the main tissue effects occur",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Cut"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Cutting"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Concentrated energy heats tissue rapidly, vaporising intracellular water and separating tissue."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Coag"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Coagulation"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Tissue heating denatures proteins and helps seal small vessels."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Bipolar"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Localised energy delivery"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Bipolar instruments concentrate the current path between their two electrodes, helping limit the area exposed to current."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "diathermy_electrocautery_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "6. Indications and surgical applications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "diathermy_electrocautery_group_9",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Specialty",
                    "Common applications"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "General surgery",
                      "Incision, dissection and haemostasis during laparotomy, hernia repair and cholecystectomy"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Obstetrics and gynaecology",
                      "Haemostasis and tissue dissection in selected procedures"
                    ],
                    [
                      "ENT",
                      "Tissue dissection and haemostasis"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Neurosurgery",
                      "Precise coagulation, often using bipolar forceps"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Urology",
                      "Tissue resection and haemostasis with compatible electrosurgical systems"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Dermatology",
                      "Removal or destruction of selected skin lesions"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Laparoscopic surgery",
                      "Cutting and coagulation using compatible instruments"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Electrosurgery is useful because it can divide tissue and control bleeding using the same platform. However, it does not reliably seal every vessel size; larger vessels may require clips, ligatures or other dedicated energy devices."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "diathermy_electrocautery_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "7. Advantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "diathermy_electrocautery_group_10",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Provides rapid tissue cutting and haemostasis.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Can reduce bleeding during dissection.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Often improves operative visibility.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Offers several tissue effects through one generator.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Many systems allow precise control of power and activation.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Bipolar instruments can provide localised coagulation without a conventional dispersive return pad.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "diathermy_electrocautery_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "8. Disadvantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "diathermy_electrocautery_group_11",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Can cause thermal injury to adjacent tissue.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Produces surgical plume, which may contain harmful particles and chemicals.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Requires training and careful equipment checks.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Incorrect settings or prolonged activation may damage tissue.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Electrical energy can cause burns, unintended activation or interference with implanted electronic devices.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "It may not be suitable near certain sensitive structures without specialised equipment and precautions.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "diathermy_electrocautery_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "9. Complications and hazards",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "diathermy_electrocautery_group_12",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/diathermy_electrocautery_10.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A. Patient burns"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "In monopolar systems, poor return-electrode contact can concentrate current at a small area and cause burns. Burns may also occur at unintended contact points or from damaged insulation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/diathermy_electrocautery_11.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "B. Surgical plume"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Thermal tissue destruction produces smoke containing fine particles and potentially hazardous chemicals. Use appropriate local smoke evacuation and respiratory protection according to policy."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/diathermy_electrocautery_12.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "C. Unintended thermal injury"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Adjacent bowel, ureters, nerves, vessels or other structures may be damaged by direct contact, lateral thermal spread or capacitive coupling in certain laparoscopic circumstances."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/diathermy_electrocautery_13.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "D. Operating-theatre fire"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An ignition source, fuel and an oxidiser can combine to cause a surgical fire. Electrosurgery can ignite alcohol-based preparations, drapes and other combustible materials, especially in oxygen-enriched environments."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/diathermy_electrocautery_14.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "E. Electromagnetic interference"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Electrosurgery may interfere with pacemakers, implantable cardioverter-defibrillators (ICDs) and other electronic devices. Appropriate perioperative assessment and device-specific precautions are required."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Other complications include bleeding due to inadequate coagulation, delayed tissue necrosis, surgical plume exposure and damage from accidental activation."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "diathermy_electrocautery_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "10. Safety precautions before, during and after use",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "diathermy_electrocautery_group_13",
              "group_title": "Before use",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Check the generator, cables, handpiece and electrodes for damage.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Confirm that the correct mode and compatible accessories are selected.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "For monopolar surgery, apply the return electrode according to the manufacturer's instructions, ensuring suitable skin contact and avoiding inappropriate placement over bony prominences, scars or compromised skin.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Check for implanted electronic devices and arrange appropriate precautions.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Allow alcohol-based skin preparations to dry completely and prevent pooling.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Assess the surgical fire risk, including the use of supplemental oxygen.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "diathermy_electrocautery_group_14",
              "group_title": "During use",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Activate the instrument only when the electrode is visible and appropriately positioned.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Use the lowest effective power setting and the shortest activation time needed.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Place the active electrode in its designated holster when not in use.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Use smoke evacuation close to the source of plume.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Avoid contact with unintended tissue and monitor the operative field for burns or fire hazards.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "diathermy_electrocautery_group_15",
              "group_title": "After use",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Deactivate the generator and follow the manufacturer's shutdown procedure.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Inspect reusable instruments and cables for damage.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Dispose of single-use electrodes and accessories correctly.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Document any equipment malfunction, burn or other adverse event and report it according to local policy.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "diathermy_electrocautery_subsection_12",
          "subsection_title": "11. Monopolar versus bipolar diathermy",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "diathermy_electrocautery_group_16",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Feature",
                    "Monopolar",
                    "Bipolar"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Current pathway",
                      "Active electrode through the patient to the return electrode",
                      "Between the two electrodes of the instrument"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Return pad",
                      "Normally required",
                      "Not normally required"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Area of current passage",
                      "Broader pathway through the patient",
                      "More localised pathway"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Common instrument",
                      "Electrosurgical pencil",
                      "Bipolar forceps"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Main strengths",
                      "Versatile cutting and coagulation",
                      "Controlled localised coagulation"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Main risks",
                      "Return-pad burns, unintended current pathways and thermal injury",
                      "Local thermal injury and damage from excessive activation"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "OSCE pearl: Bipolar does not mean risk-free. Excessive energy can still cause thermal damage, and current may spread through tissue beyond the immediate grasping point."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "diathermy_electrocautery_subsection_13",
          "subsection_title": "12. Diathermy versus electrocautery",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "diathermy_electrocautery_group_17",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "This is a classic viva question."
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Feature",
                    "Diathermy / electrosurgery",
                    "Electrocautery"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Principle",
                      "High-frequency electrical current passes through tissue",
                      "Electric current heats an element, which transfers heat to tissue"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Current through patient",
                      "Yes, in electrosurgery",
                      "No, not as part of the heating mechanism"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Tissue cutting",
                      "Common with electrosurgical systems",
                      "Depends on the device; conventional cautery is mainly used for heat application"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Haemostasis",
                      "Yes",
                      "Yes"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Examples",
                      "Monopolar pencil, bipolar forceps",
                      "Battery-operated or electrically heated cautery pen"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Do not confuse either with ultrasonic instruments, which use mechanical vibration to produce tissue effects, or radiofrequency ablation, which is a distinct energy-based treatment technique."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "diathermy_electrocautery_subsection_14",
          "subsection_title": "13. High-yield OSCE viva questions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "diathermy_electrocautery_group_18",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. What is diathermy?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The application of high-frequency electrical energy to tissue to produce cutting, coagulation or other controlled thermal effects."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. Why is high-frequency alternating current used?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It permits tissue heating while reducing the nerve and muscle stimulation associated with ordinary low-frequency electrical current."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. What is the purpose of the patient return electrode?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It safely returns current to the generator in a conventional monopolar circuit by distributing it over an appropriate contact area."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. Why is bipolar diathermy preferred for certain delicate procedures?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The current is concentrated between the two electrodes, allowing localised coagulation and avoiding the need for a conventional monopolar return pad."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. What causes a return-electrode burn?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Inadequate contact or an unsuitable contact area can increase current density at the skin, resulting in excessive heating. Incorrect placement and equipment problems can also contribute."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. Why is surgical smoke evacuation necessary?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Surgical plume can contain fine particles and potentially hazardous chemicals. Source-capture evacuation reduces exposure to the surgical team."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. What are the three components of the fire triangle?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An ignition source, fuel and an oxidiser. Electrosurgery can provide ignition, while drapes or alcohol-based preparations can provide fuel and oxygen-enriched conditions can accelerate combustion."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q8. What precautions are required for a patient with a pacemaker or ICD?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Identify the device, assess the procedure and anticipated electromagnetic interference, and follow an appropriate device-specific perioperative plan. Monitoring and device reprogramming or other precautions may be necessary."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
